      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, thanks for clarifying. I just wanted to be sure I wasn't missing some niche academic theory. \n\nGoing back to the more concrete stuff though—since you mentioned there were merchants and groups that managed these risks—could you tell me more about how they actually handled \"theft\" or \"unaccounted loss\"? Like, if a caravan arrived and a significant portion of the goods had just vanished without any visible sign of a robbery, how did the merchants or the local authorities usually investigate or explain that happening in such remote areas?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Investigating unexplained losses in the vast, often lawless stretches of the Silk Road was a monumental task, far more complex than a simple \"missing inventory\" report. When goods vanished without visible signs of a typical bandit raid, it was often attributed to a mix of environmental factors, internal disputes, or the subtle machinations of local powers. Merchants often relied on networks of trusted guides, who acted as informal investigators, tracking unusual movements or suspicious local behavior. Sometimes, the \"loss\" was simply a sophisticated form of tax evasion or extortion by local strongmen who operated outside formal authority.",
      "source": "target",
